WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:25.920
Thank you so much for making&nbsp;
time for Deutsche Welle.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:31.840
So, some say these are the most important&nbsp;
elections this year do you agree?

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:38.640
Absolutely the Budestag election on September&nbsp;
26 is the most important election happening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:44.000
anywhere in the world this year, in my opinion&nbsp;
because Germany is at the heart of Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.280
Germany, defines in many ways that Europe takes,&nbsp;
and it's a crucial part of the transatlantic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:58.000
bond. So, this is undoubtedly going to be&nbsp;
a major event on the political landscape.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.440
Germany has a very different system than&nbsp;
the United States we have several parties,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:09.200
not only two is here, maybe you can&nbsp;
talk a little bit why that matters.

00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:16.000
Sure. A parliamentary system in which you&nbsp;
have many parties represented more parties&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:22.560
now than ever before in Germany, in recent German&nbsp;
history. It means that any government has to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:28.000
formed as a coalition, nobody has&nbsp;
an absolute majority on, on his own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:33.440
and therefore, that means compromise, so it&nbsp;
means that nobody gets exactly what they want,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:39.520
and it means also that people in the United&nbsp;
States who are thinking about the impact on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.840
the German American relationship have to&nbsp;
take into account this coalition dynamic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:49.040
this compromise which is going to characterize&nbsp;
German politics in the future as it always has.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.560
What does that mean for reliability?

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.200
The, the tendency in Germany to have a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:02.960
negotiated coalition agreement. It gives a&nbsp;
certain amount of predictability. And I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.640
say the problem is, in some ways, perhaps in the&nbsp;
opposite direction. Because parties in Germany,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.360 --> 00:02:14.160
when they form a government. They have a written&nbsp;
coalition agreement which is often dozens,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:20.800
if not more pages long, there's a desire to&nbsp;
try to anticipate it. You can't anticipate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.800 --> 00:02:28.640
everything. And so it's that, that can give a&nbsp;
lack of flexibility to future actions, because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:32.960
things that are not covered in the coalition&nbsp;
agreement then have to be hammered out a new,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:38.480
and there can be a reluctance to deviate from&nbsp;
those agreements, so I think it's, it's more a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:45.040
question of having enough flexibility to react&nbsp;
to the way the world is going to change, rather&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:50.320
than predictability about what your positions&nbsp;
are today, and for the next couple of months.

00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:56.080
As you're an expert on international affairs,&nbsp;
maybe we can talk a little bit about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.080 --> 00:02:58.720
expectations of the three biggest parties.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:04.080
I think that Chancellor Merkel&nbsp;
having been in power for 16 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:11.520
there is an expectation of a certain continuity.&nbsp;
And there you see from an American perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.040
that the chancellor has been on the one hand,&nbsp;
concerned about holding Europe together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.200
the European Union, especially&nbsp;
through a number of crises. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:28.560
Germany as the largest and the richest country in&nbsp;
Europe has the delicate task of trying to bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:35.840
together the ambitions of those who want to more&nbsp;
European integration and they wanted faster with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:41.280
those who are more skeptical, and in that sense,&nbsp;
Germany is a compromise broker rather than a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:48.400
at the vanguard like new innovations, instead&nbsp;
it's sort of a moderating force. And I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:54.720
that when you look at Europe, Armin laschet The&nbsp;
Chancellor candidate from the CDU/CSU has, has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.720 --> 00:04:00.080
said that he will think, European and he will act&nbsp;
European, and he has a European instinct. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:07.040
I think that would be something to look for from a&nbsp;
government that he might lead. How will it seek to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.040 --> 00:04:13.520
strengthen the European level of policymaking and&nbsp;
what concessions will Germany be willing to offer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:19.920
its European partners in order to achieve that&nbsp;
greater degree of cohesion. Will that be on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:25.680
financial or fiscal side, will that be in the&nbsp;
foreign policy, or will it be in other areas.

00:04:26.320 --> 00:04:32.800
As for now it looks as you just said very likely&nbsp;
that he might be the successor of Angela Merkel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.800 --> 00:04:36.320
talking a little bit about his take&nbsp;
on the transatlantic relationship.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:44.400
If you talk about the transatlantic relationship,&nbsp;
the CDU and CSU, have always tried to to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.400 --> 00:04:51.200
characterize themselves as, as real advocate&nbsp;
staunch advocates of a strong transatlantic bond.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.920 --> 00:04:58.000
And that has held, regardless of the President&nbsp;
in power in the United States, there have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:05.760
major ups and downs in the relationship, but that&nbsp;
that real adherence to the transatlantic pillar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.480 --> 00:05:12.240
as one of the two most important aspects of German&nbsp;
foreign policy. On the one hand, the United States&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:19.200
on the other hand, the European Union is, is at&nbsp;
the center of the CDU’s view of the world. 
 
 &nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:26.480
Let's talk a little bit about Olof Scholz from&nbsp;
the Social Democrats, they have a traditionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:31.200
closer relationship to Russia&nbsp;
Gerhard Schroder one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:38.320
Chancellor's from the Social Democrats, was kind&nbsp;
of the mastermind behind, behind Nordstream 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.960
What what is your take on Olof Scholz when&nbsp;
it comes to the German American relationship.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:49.680
I think Olof Scholz, if you listen&nbsp;
to the way he has portrayed his view&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.680 --> 00:05:56.160
of the world and of Germany's place in the world.&nbsp;
And if you look at the way he has talked about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.160 --> 00:06:03.680
the Biden administration. I think he is&nbsp;
sought to align them quite closely to tell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.720
German voters in a way I know how to work with&nbsp;
the US administration under President Joe Biden.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:18.000
And and I think that encompasses not only some&nbsp;
of the traditional foreign policy elements,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.960
I think we'll come back in a second to&nbsp;
Russia and to NordStream, but it's also about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:29.040
the global economy. What kind of regulation should&nbsp;
there be in the global economy, Olof Scholz, along&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.040 --> 00:06:36.720
with the United States Government have talked a&nbsp;
lot about the minimum tax on the international&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:43.680
corporations, and that has been achieved an&nbsp;
agreement in the g7 context also in the G 20&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.680 --> 00:06:47.760
Now it's a question of turning that into&nbsp;
national obligations, but I think there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:53.440
you see an area where the Social Democrats&nbsp;
have tried to present themselves in a way as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.240 --> 00:07:00.480
transatlantic-assists for the global digitized&nbsp;
global economy. Now when it comes to Russia I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.480 --> 00:07:03.920
think you're absolutely right, people in the&nbsp;
United States, when you look at the Russia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.920 --> 00:07:10.960
policies of Germany and Europe. One of the first&nbsp;
things they focus on is the NordStream 2 pipeline&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.960 --> 00:07:17.840
project. And and I think Chancellor Merkel,&nbsp;
of course has defended that project, and she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:24.880
has seen through an agreement with the United&nbsp;
States about how that might move forward. But I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:31.360
think people still see that as primarily a social&nbsp;
democratic party initiative, and the priority and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.360 --> 00:07:36.960
there I think there's just a major disagreement&nbsp;
in both American parties, the Democrats and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.960 --> 00:07:43.840
Republicans are quite critical of Nord Stream&nbsp;
two, and, and that stance isn't going to change,

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:52.000
Then there are the greens, there were times when&nbsp;
people were expecting hoping fearing that Annalena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.000 --> 00:07:57.120
Baerbock might become the next chancellor.  
 
 
What I think you see from an American perspective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.120 --> 00:08:04.000
is how strongly the values orientation comes&nbsp;
through in the greens, foreign policy vision.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:07.920
You can see that when you look at&nbsp;
questions that relate to China,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:15.360
and especially things like forced labor, and&nbsp;
the importance of human rights, including&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:20.720
the implications of human rights policy for&nbsp;
Germany's international economic engagement&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.720 --> 00:08:26.640
there I think the greens are they tried to go&nbsp;
much further than than other parties in Germany&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.640 --> 00:08:32.960
have gone thus far. If you look at Russia policy,&nbsp;
I think you see also a not only a criticism of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.960 --> 00:08:42.320
Russian foreign policy, but more fundamentally&nbsp;
a deep disappointment but a deep concern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:46.880
about human rights in Russia about&nbsp;
the nature of Vladimir Putin's regime,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:53.040
and about the implications that has for stability&nbsp;
in Russia and Russia's role in its immediate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.040 --> 00:08:58.680
neighborhood. And more broadly, in Europe and&nbsp;
in the world. So I think there, you see a values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:05.360
driven alignment that is very similar to the way&nbsp;
Joe Biden talks about the competition between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.360 --> 00:09:11.600
democracies and autocracies as one of the defining&nbsp;
elements of the international situation today.

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:18.160
Do you have an idea, of whom the Biden&nbsp;
administration would kind of prefer.

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:25.200
Well, I think they would be careful never&nbsp;
to express a preference because there's,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:31.200
there's, there's nothing to be gained by trying&nbsp;
to weigh in, in advance of an election. It can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:38.080
only backfire and be used against you. I think&nbsp;
that what people in the Biden administration,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:44.800
recognize, is that any German government&nbsp;
is going to be the result of compromise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.800 --> 00:09:49.920
and concessions, among the partners. So&nbsp;
that's the first thing is don't read the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.920 --> 00:09:57.840
party program of one party and assume that that&nbsp;
will that will shape the Germany of the future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.840 --> 00:10:05.120
but I think you can also look at these positions&nbsp;
and see how Germany's foreign policy might evolve.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:11.440
And there, I think you can find something, you&nbsp;
know something for everyone. If you it on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:17.520
one hand the continuity and the transatlantic&nbsp;
commitment in the CDU CSU approach. On the other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:25.440
hand, the views of the global economy, that the&nbsp;
Social Democrats, bring or the the values driven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:35.360
imperative of the greens program. So I think&nbsp;
people will be looking for new leadership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.360 --> 00:10:42.720
new personalities and trying to forge constructive&nbsp;
working partnerships with whoever emerges. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:49.280
we also we haven't talked about the Liberals the&nbsp;
Free Democrats. There's also I think a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.280 --> 00:10:55.120
likelihood that they might be part of a future&nbsp;
German government, and there I think you also see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.080 --> 00:11:06.560
a, you see a bit of the values basis in the FDPs&nbsp;
election program. And, and I think it's a reminder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.120 --> 00:11:12.000
that this may be the most complicated German&nbsp;
government that we've seen in recent history.

00:11:12.000 --> 00:11:12.640
IP:

00:11:12.640 --> 00:11:18.320
I think we have to talk a little bit at the end of&nbsp;
this interview about Angela Merkel after 16 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.320 --> 00:11:25.200
in office that's a long period she shaped the&nbsp;
understanding of Germany for a whole generation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.200 --> 00:11:28.560
generation I think also in the&nbsp;
United States, she's very well known.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:32.080
What is her legacy when it comes&nbsp;
to the transatlantic relationship,

00:11:32.080 --> 00:11:32.880
How long do we have.

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:37.120
let's say two minutes.

00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:45.040
Okay, two minutes. So I think that Merkel's&nbsp;
legacy is, you know, it is on the one hand, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:51.760
commitment to preserving a working German American&nbsp;
partnership and a transatlantic relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.760 --> 00:11:58.480
you see this in the way she took heat at the start&nbsp;
of her chancellorship for finding ways to work&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.480 --> 00:12:05.200
closely with President George W Bush, America's&nbsp;
popularity in Germany was at a low point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:13.280
when she took office in 2005. And so, you know,&nbsp;
it's important to remember that she has stood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:19.280
for a strong partnership with the United States,&nbsp;
even when it was unpopular. I think you see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:25.440
as well during the presidency of Donald Trump's,&nbsp;
although in that case, you see Merkel I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:32.720
drawing distinctions, to a greater degree between&nbsp;
German policies, German disagreements with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:32.720 --> 00:12:38.160
United States, while at the same time recognizing&nbsp;
the essential importance of that relationship.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:38.960 --> 00:12:45.280
And then, I think you also have to look at&nbsp;
her commitment to keeping the European Union,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.160 --> 00:12:52.320
functional, and together on major issues. That is&nbsp;
a hugely important for the United States as well.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.960 --> 00:12:58.960
Chaos in Europe is not good for the United States,&nbsp;
that does not benefit us, and having someone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:07.680
is able to take the long view, and to foster a&nbsp;
strong European Union and and a strong Europeans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.680 --> 00:13:13.360
contribution to transatlantic security in&nbsp;
NATO is really crucially important to me.

00:13:13.360 --> 00:13:16.480
Jeffrey, thank you so much, that&nbsp;
shall be continued after the election&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:20.880
and then we discuss the outcome and the&nbsp;
implication for the US-German relationship.

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:31.724
I look forward to it. Thanks so much., Ines.

